UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 10, Lesson 2: Editing and publishing the report', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Before sharing an information report, revise factual organisation and evidence, then proofread grammar, punctuation, spelling, headings and captions.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCorrect the language in ''The animal have thick fur and it sleep in trees''; identify what facts would need checking.\n\nFEEDBACK\n''The animal has thick fur and sleeps in trees.'' Whether those claims are true depends on the animal and must be verified.\n\nCHECK\nA spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRevise your earlier report or a supplied draft in two passes: factual meaning and structure first; sentence-level editing second. Produce a publishable 200-word version with source notes, headings and caption. Record three substantive changes and three proofread changes.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Before sharing an information report, revise factual organisation and evidence, then proofread grammar, punctuation, spelling, headings and captions.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-CWT-02"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Revise your earlier report or a supplied draft in two passes: factual meaning and structure first; sentence-level editing second. Produce a publishable 200-word version with source notes, headings and caption. Record thr Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: ''The animal has thick fur and sleeps in trees.'' Whether those claims are true depends on the animal and must be verified. Reasoning: I used revise facts by attending to proofread and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: A spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram. Correction: ''The animal has thick fur and sleeps in trees.'' Whether those claims are true depends on the animal and must be verified. Why: the correction restores accurate use of revise facts.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating revise facts on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with revise facts, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"''The animal has thick fur and sleeps in trees.'' Whether those claims are true depends on the animal and must be verified. This matches the lesson skill for revise facts.","options":["A spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","''The animal has thick fur and sleeps in trees.'' Whether those claims are true depends on the animal and must be verified."],"question":"Correct the language in ''The animal have thick fur and it sleep in trees''; identify what facts would need checking. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Before sharing an information report, revise factual organisation and evidence, then proofread grammar, punctuation, spelling, headings and captions.","''The animal has thick fur and sleeps in trees.'' Whether those claims are true depends on the animal and must be verified.","Careful use of proofread and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","A spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising revise facts?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named proofread and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the proofread and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"A learner is practising revise facts. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries revise facts.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show revise facts and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","A spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram."],"question":"Read: \"Beside the oval, Kai practised bowling until the sun slipped low.\" Which choice best uses revise facts from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["A spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","''The animal has thick fur and sleeps in trees.'' Whether those claims are true depends on the animal and must be verified."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about revise facts?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise proofread and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["A spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about revise facts.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of revise facts to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating revise facts on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: ''The animal has thick fur and sleeps in trees.'' Whether those claims are true depends on the animal and must be verified. Reasoning: I used revise facts by attending to proofread and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with revise facts, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: A spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram. Correction: ''The animal has thick fur and sleeps in trees.'' Whether those claims are true depends on the animal and must be verified. Why: the correction restores accurate use of revise facts.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w10-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise revise facts.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use revise facts with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify proofread and explain why it matters.","I can practise publish independently on a new prompt.","I can notice source note needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Before sharing an information report, revise factual organisation and evidence, then proofread grammar, punctuation, spelling, headings and captions. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Before sharing an information report, revise factual organisation and evidence, then proofread grammar, punctuation, spelling, headings and captions."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about revise facts?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDraft: ''Koalas eats leaves. They live all over the world.'' Revision: ''Koalas eat mainly eucalypt leaves. They occur naturally in parts of eastern Australia.'' I would verify range and diet with an authoritative source before publishing.\n\nTRY FIRST\nCorrect the language in ''The animal have thick fur and it sleep in trees''; identify what facts would need checking.\n\nFEEDBACK\n''The animal has thick fur and sleeps in trees.'' Whether those claims are true depends on the animal and must be verified.","explain":"Before sharing an information report, revise factual organisation and evidence, then proofread grammar, punctuation, spelling, headings and captions.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Grammar and fact revision of a koala diet sentence.","caption":"Revise meaning and evidence before surface proofreading.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Draft error</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Koalas eats leaves</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Revised fact</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">eat eucalypt leaves</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Draft: ''Koalas eats leaves. They live all over the world.'' Revision: ''Koalas eat mainly eucalypt leaves. They occur natu Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Draft: ''Koalas eats leaves. They live all over the world.'' Revision: ''Koalas eat mainly eucalypt leaves. They occur natu Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about revise facts?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDraft: ''Koalas eats leaves. They live all over the world.'' Revision: ''Koalas eat mainly eucalypt leaves. They occur naturally in parts of eastern Australia.'' I would verify range and diet with an authoritative source before publishing. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Factual revision pass beside a sentence-editing pass.","caption":"Spell tools cannot prove a caption or fact is true.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Meaning pass</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">confirm range claim</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Edit pass</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">capitals and verbs</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about revise facts?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram. Here is a more productive model: Draft: ''Koalas eats leaves. They live all over the world.'' Revision: ''Koalas eat mainly eucalypt leaves. They occur naturally in parts of eastern Australia.'' I would verify range and diet with an authoritative source before publishing.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\n''The animal has thick fur and sleeps in trees.'' Whether those claims are true depends on the animal and must be verified.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. A spellchecker cannot establish that a factual claim is accurate or that a caption matches a diagram. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about revise facts?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBefore sharing an information report, revise factual organisation and evidence, then proofread grammar, punctuation, spelling, headings and captions. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 10, Lesson 2: Editing and publishing the report","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Information reports","week":10,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nDraft: ''Koalas eats leaves. They live all over the world.'' Revision: ''Koalas eat mainly eucalypt leaves. They occur naturally in parts of eastern Australia.'' I would verify range and diet with an authoritative source before publishing.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Grammar and fact revision of a koala diet sentence.","caption":"Revise meaning and evidence before surface proofreading.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Draft erro